            <ArticleTitle>Gabapentin-induced aquagenic wrinkling of the palms.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">13030/qt64k739q5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Aquagenic keratoderma (AK) or aquagenic wrinklingis a rare palmoplantar skin disease. It is sporadic orhereditary condition. It appears in childhood or youngadulthood and it is seen as multiple asymptomaticsmall shiny papules on the peripheral margin ofpalms and/or soles after submersion in water. Thepathogenesis and etiology of ASA remains unclear.Drugs sometimes trigger AK. Herein, we present thecase of a 29-year-old man who had begun treatmentwith gabapentin three weeks before the onset of hiscutaneous symptoms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
